From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Matkovich <sebastian.matkovich@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 07:56:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: omap3-n950: headphone jack detection

The N950 detects a plugged headphone jack on TWL5031 GPIO 0 (AvPlugDet),
low when plugged and pulled up, as Nokia's RM-680 board file and its
TWL5031 ACI driver have it. A gpio-keys switch reports it as
SW_HEADPHONE_INSERT, which is what user space (Leste's headphoned,
PulseAudio) looks for. Headset microphones and the headset button go
through the ACI block, which has no mainline driver.

Untested on hardware.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm/boot/dts/ti/omap/omap3-n950.dts | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm/boot/dts/ti/omap/omap3-n950.dts b/arch/arm/boot/dts/ti/omap/omap3-n950.dts
index cf545287a..d2c7bf8ea 100644
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/ti/omap/omap3-n950.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/ti/omap/omap3-n950.dts
@@ -39,6 +39,26 @@ keypad_slide {
 			pinctrl-0 = <&keypad_slide_pins>;
 		};
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * The headphone jack's plug detection (AvPlugDet) on TWL5031 GPIO 0,
+	 * low when plugged, pulled up (Nokia's board file; its ACI driver
+	 * reads the line the same way). Telling headsets from headphones and
+	 * their button takes that driver, which mainline has not.
+	 */
+	jack {
+		compatible = "gpio-keys";
+		label = "Headphone Jack";
+
+		switch-headphone {
+			label = "Headphone";
+			gpios = <&twl_gpio 0 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+			linux,input-type = <EV_SW>;
+			linux,code = <SW_HEADPHONE_INSERT>;
+			debounce-interval = <50>;
+			wakeup-source;
+		};
+	};
 };
 
 &omap3_pmx_core {
